## 2. Normalizing flows for the causal graph — DONE (2026-07-31), edgeless graph

> Second, I need to finally explicitly add the Normalizing flows for the causal graph to the model. Note that you should pay attention to normalization over dataset, needs for normalizing flow training, being general so it is dataset agnostic and ...

Goal: replace the current editing scheme (flip one attribute's conditioning index,
independently of the rest) with real structural-causal-model counterfactuals: abduct
exogenous noise per attribute, intervene on the target, propagate the predicted effect to
causal descendants via normalizing flows, then condition HDAE on the full resulting
attribute vector.

What landed:
- `experiments/hdae/configs/causal_graph.yaml`: shared, model-agnostic DAG config
  (`attributes`, `edges`, `logit_smoothing_eps`, `scm_checkpoint`) — mirrors
  `cohorts.yaml`. **Your call on the DAG: edges = [] (fully independent) for now** —
  add `[parent, child]` pairs here later to activate propagation; nothing else changes.
- `experiments/hdae/causal/graph.py`: `CausalGraph` (parents/children/transitive
  descendants/topological order, Kahn's-algorithm cycle detection).
- `experiments/hdae/causal/normalize.py`: logit-of-smoothed-probability transform
  (resolves the "CelebA attributes are binary" open decision below) — `eps` lives in
  the config, not hardcoded.
- `experiments/hdae/causal/scm.py`: per-node conditional Gaussian in logit space
  (`nflows`'s `ConditionalDiagonalNormal` + identity transform — a 1-D scalar target
  can't support a coupling/autoregressive transform, so this is the simplest valid
  instance of the "Deep SCM" pattern; richer transforms are a drop-in for a future
  multi-dimensional node). `fit`/`abduct`/`propagate`/`counterfactual_binary`,
  following Pearl's abduct-intervene-predict recipe.
- `experiments/hdae/causal/train_scm.py`: fits on the packed attribute table only
  (dataset-agnostic, no image code) — **note:** currently fits on the same images later
  drawn into CF1 eval cohorts (train/eval leakage for the SCM specifically, not
  HDAE/the classifier); low-stakes with an edgeless graph, worth a held-out split if
  real edges get added.
- `experiments/hdae/causal/verify_scm.py`: **the actual acceptance test** — the shipped
  edgeless graph doesn't exercise `propagate()` at all (every node's descendant set is
  empty), so this fits a toy non-empty chain (`Male -> Young -> Smiling`) on the real
  packed data and asserts round-trip identity, correct 2-hop propagation, and
  non-descendant isolation. All three pass.
- Integration: `CFModelAdapter.intervene()` (item 1's contract) gained a required
  `cf_attrs` param — the SCM's full propagated counterfactual vector, keyed by
  attribute name — `HDAEAdapter` uses it to set the whole `y_idx` row (not just the
  flipped column); `DiffAEProbeAdapter` ignores it (no attribute-vector conditioning).
  `run_cf1_eval.py::evaluate_intervention` computes `cf_attrs` via
  `scm.counterfactual_binary()` every batch and passes it down — no `None`/fallback
  path, if the SCM checkpoint is missing it fails loudly (`SCM.load` -> `torch.load`).
  With the shipped edgeless graph this is a verified no-op vs. item 1's behavior (same
  CC on every row of a real smoke run) — ready for real edges without further code
  changes.
- This also drove the CF1 metric split into observed/unobserved and CC's extension to
  descendants — see item 4 below (they landed together, same commit range).

Depends on: item 1 (`intervene()` takes the full attribute vector) — done, in order.

Open decisions, resolved:
- The DAG itself — **edges = [] for now**, your explicit call.
- Flow library — `nflows` (installed; matches the plan's leaning).
- Binary attributes — logit-of-smoothed-probability, `eps` in config.


